# As L5_14_3_flows_of_plan, with a raise of amt to 15 governing 2026-01.. known 2026-02-15: rent[2026-01] and
# rent[2026-02], booked before, change after they were paid, and no move settles them, so every flows that counts
# them from 2026-02-15 on is HOLE-CHANGED (L §6.5 change 3: "Without the two moves, every flows that could count the
# adjustments ... is a hole asking whether back pay is due"; E §5 steps 3-6).  echo_pay[2026-02], re-evaluated at
# 2026-02-15 (E §5 step 3), reads flows(rent, 2026-02), now that hole, so it changes too.  What matters here: the read
# measures rent's adjustments, not a pass over every plan that would reach echo_pay itself (no RUN-CYCLE).
=== m.tally
model m
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-15
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-06-30
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01
place konto: accounts PLN lives 2026-01-01..
place world: outside lives 2026-01-01..
index month = month 2026-01..2026-06
input amt: money PLN
move rent[m in month]: transfers
  on start_of(m)
  at start_of(m)
  leg cash from konto to world amount amt
move echo_pay[m in month]: transfers
  on add_days(start_of(m), 1)
  at start_of(m)
  leg cash from konto to world amount flows(rent, m)
source s "x"
version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2025-12-31
  amt = 10
  opening[konto] = 0 at 2026-01-01 start
ask q: flows(echo_pay, 2026), flows(rent, 2026) law cut

version raise
  cite s
  governs 2026-01..
  known 2026-02-15
  replace amt = 15
=== expect
q: (?"echo_pay[2026-02] on 2026-02-02 changed after it was paid (version raise); is back pay due, and when?" ...
...
